.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt SPLICE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm splice ,
.Nm tee ,
.Nm vmsplice ,
.Nm sendfile ,
.Nm copy_file_range
.Nd copy between descriptors in the kernel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn splice "int fd_in" "off_t *off_in" "int fd_out" "off_t *off_out" "size_t len" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn tee "int fd_in" "int fd_out" "size_t len" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn vmsplice "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "size_t nr_segs" "unsigned int flags"
.In sys/sendfile.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn sendfile "int out_fd" "int in_fd" "off_t *offset" "size_t count"
.In unistd.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn copy_file_range "int fd_in" "off_t *off_in" "int fd_out" "off_t *off_out" "size_t len" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls move bytes from one descriptor to another without copying
them through the caller.
Linux moves pages; this kernel copies through a buffer of its own, 32 KiB
at a time, by the same paths
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
take, so a destination behaves as it does for a write: a file's offset,
size limit and set-ID bits, a socket's
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
a pipe's readers.
A call that has moved a byte stops at a signal and returns the count.
Each count is at most 2147479552 bytes, as for
.Xr read 2 .
.Pp
.Fn splice
moves up to
.Fa len
bytes between two descriptors of which at least one is a pipe.
From a pipe to a pipe the bytes go from ring to ring.
From a pipe to anything else they are written as
.Xr write 2
writes them, and only the bytes written leave the pipe, so a short or
failed write leaves the rest there.
From a regular file to a pipe it reads what fits in the pipe.
A pipe of
.Em n
pages holds at most
.Em n
of Linux's page buffers, and is full for
.Fn splice ,
.Fn tee ,
.Fn vmsplice
and
.Xr poll 2
once it has that many, however few bytes they hold: each page of a file
or of the caller's memory that the bytes come from takes one, and so does
each buffer of another pipe, so a pipe of 64 KiB takes sixteen one-byte
.Fn vmsplice
calls, or 65535 bytes from byte 1 of a page.
For the side that is a regular file, a non-null
.Fa off_in
or
.Fa off_out
gives the offset to read or write at, which the call moves past the bytes
and which leaves the description's offset alone; a null one uses the
description's offset and moves it.
A pipe end waits for bytes or for room, before the first byte only,
unless
.Dv SPLICE_F_NONBLOCK
is given or its description has
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ;
the call then fails with
.Er EAGAIN
instead.
.Dv SPLICE_F_MOVE ,
.Dv SPLICE_F_MORE
and
.Dv SPLICE_F_GIFT
are taken and change nothing.
A source other than a pipe or a regular file, a socket among them, is
.Er EINVAL ,
where Linux reads a socket.
.Pp
.Fn tee
copies up to
.Fa len
bytes from one pipe to another and leaves them in the first, waiting as
.Fn splice
does.
.Pp
.Fn vmsplice
copies the
.Fa nr_segs
buffers of
.Fa iov
into the pipe of the write end
.Fa fd ,
as
.Xr writev 2
would, or the pipe of a read end into the buffers; a descriptor open both
ways writes.
Linux hands the pipe the caller's pages; this kernel copies the bytes,
which a caller that leaves a buffer alone after
.Dv SPLICE_F_GIFT
cannot tell apart.
The pipe end waits as
.Fn splice
does: writing, the call takes what fits once it has copied a byte;
reading, only the first buffer waits, and the call stops at an empty pipe
once it has copied a byte.
A page Linux would not hand the pipe, as its
.Fn get_user_pages
refuses it, is not copied either: the vDSO's data page,
.Li [vvar] ,
a region of
.Xr memfd_secret 2 ,
device memory or a page the caller cannot read ends the call, with the
bytes before it or
.Er EFAULT .
The vector is checked whole first, as
.Xr readv 2
checks it.
.Pp
.Fn sendfile
copies up to
.Fa count
bytes of the regular file open on
.Fa in_fd
to
.Fa out_fd ,
which may be any descriptor open for writing: a socket, a file, a pipe or
a terminal.
A non-null
.Fa offset
gives the offset to read at and is moved past the bytes sent; a null one
uses the input's offset and moves it.
A pipe takes what fits in it.
.Pp
.Fn copy_file_range
copies up to
.Fa len
bytes from one regular file to another, with offsets as
.Fn splice
has them for a file, and stops at the end of the input.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
No file system here shares extents between files, so every copy is the
generic one Linux falls back to, between any two mounts.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 313 for
.Fn splice ,
315 for
.Fn tee ,
316 for
.Fn vmsplice ,
187 for
.Fn sendfile
with a 32-bit offset and 239 for
.Fn sendfile64
with a 64-bit one, and 377 for
.Fn copy_file_range .
musl's
.Fn sendfile
uses 239.
On x86-64 they are 275, 276, 278, 40 and 326, every offset is 64 bits, and
there is no
.Nm sendfile64 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each call returns the bytes moved, 0 for a length of 0, for an empty pipe
with no writer left and for an input offset at or past the end of a
file, or -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
moves a pipe's bytes to
.Pa /dev/null
and a file's bytes from an offset into a pipe with
.Fn splice ,
copies a pipe's bytes to another with
.Fn tee
and finds them still in the first, sends a file to an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket and to a file with
.Fn sendfile ,
and copies a file's bytes into another with
.Fn copy_file_range .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOVERFLOW]"
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A pipe end would wait, with
.Dv SPLICE_F_NONBLOCK
or
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
or a socket output has
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
and no room.
.It Bq Er EBADF
A descriptor is not open, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
or is not open for its direction; for
.Fn vmsplice ,
also a descriptor that is no pipe; for
.Fn copy_file_range ,
also an output with
.Dv O_APPEND .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
An offset cannot be read or written, or a
.Fn vmsplice
vector or buffer cannot be reached or holds a page Linux would not hand
the pipe.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal came before a byte moved.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A flag is unknown, a
.Fn vmsplice
vector has more than 1024 buffers or a length negative as a signed size,
neither
.Fn splice
descriptor is a pipe, both are one pipe, a
.Fn tee
descriptor is no pipe, a source is not a regular file or a pipe, an
offset is below 0 or is given for an output that does not seek, or an
output of
.Fn splice
or
.Fn sendfile
has
.Dv O_APPEND ;
for
.Fn copy_file_range ,
also ranges of one file that overlap.
.It Bq Er EISDIR
A
.Fn copy_file_range
descriptor is a directory.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel has no room for the buffer.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The 32-bit offset of i386's
.Fn sendfile
is at 2147483647, or a
.Fn copy_file_range
range passes the largest offset.
.It Bq Er EPIPE
A pipe output has no reader left;
.Dv SIGPIPE
is sent too.
.It Bq Er ESPIPE
An offset is given for a pipe.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2
